eliminating redundant degrees of freedom in scientific simulations by incorporating random variables into the model. While randomization enhances unpredictability, it also makes simulation outcomes less reliable. Therefore, determining the optimal level of randomization is crucial to balance realism and computational efficiency. This study examines a novel probabilistic model for downscaling precipitation. By incorporating two distinct types of randomization – one into the climate model and another into the surface layer – we analyze their effects on simulated precipitation patterns. We evaluated these effects using a new metric that quantifies the similarity between simulated and observed precipitation patterns, and discovered that randomization into the climate model is more beneficial. We also explored how the level of randomization affects the realism of the simulations and computational efficiency, and found an optimal range of randomization for best results. This study provides a framework for identifying and validating the optimal range of randomization for various downscaling models. The approach employed in this research can also be applied to other downscaling models and fields involving complex systems. The code and data from this research are available online at https://github.com/wclark3/DGCM-downscaling. Additionally, a pre-print of this study has been published at https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10384. \\ \newpage{}  
